      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5f2ed19f8fc2c972dd4b6a4c|full_name} \n\nI knew the article and technique, but I think the technique is not a problem.\n\nMost popular/practical use cases are not increasing drawing pixels and abuse of this requires shader hack, which can use in-shader multi pass rendering.\n\nIn general multi-pass rendering cannot draw polygons substantially different from original model, except for new, abusing shaders.\n\nThe most practical use case is stencil as you linked, that skips drawing many pixels.\n\nThe reason why AAO increase triangles on manual merge is no other way to replicate the behavior for non-last material slots except for modifying shader, and modifying shader is not implemented in AAO. (FYI, AAO's automatic merge does not do such merge)",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
